</mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    Mojtaba takes power
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="Mojtaba takes power"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What happened?</strong> Iran’s regime has named <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c78xxg05w0zo" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">Mojtaba Khamenei the Islamic Republic’s supreme leader</a>, succeeding his father, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. Iran's Revolutionary Guard issued a statement expressing support, as did the Iran-backed Lebanese militant group Hezbollah. Some observe this shift as an attempt to elevate his religious standing and present him as a credible leader.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Mojtaba’s background:</strong> Born in the northeastern city of Mashhad, Mojtaba is the second of Khamenei's six children. At age 17, Mojtaba served in the military for several short periods during the Iran-Iraq War. Unlike his father, the 56-year-old has largely kept a low profile. He has never held government office, nor given public speeches or interviews. Mojtaba remains a mid-ranking cleric, which could pose an obstacle to his ascension to the supreme leadership.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Criticism: </strong>For years, there have been rumours that Mojtaba held considerable influence behind the scenes in Iran. His name surfaced during the 2005 election that brought Mahmoud Ahmadinejad to power and the <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2013/6/12/what-happened-to-the-green-movement-in-iran" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">2009 Green Movement protests</a>, when reformists accused him of wielding quiet power. Expected to uphold hardline policies amid economic woes, his untested leadership and recent "Ayatollah" elevation spark debate over legitimacy.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

<mj-spacer height="10px" />
